=== w15-crunch
_note: DEADLINE WEEK: conference (chairs.json stages.conf, deadline 15). The conference roll in resolveBills is AUTOMATIC and halves a bill on a miss — the highest-stakes check in the game and the only one the player never opts into — so this scene exists to make the player feel it coming rather than discover it in a status word. Grange returns here because conference is where the business roundtable's access is worth the most and the coalition's is worth the least.
talks: grange, coalition, office
actions: rally, lobby

--- start
Conference. Room 309, and you are not in it.
Six people — three from each chamber, named Monday — sitting at a table with the House draft on one side and the Senate draft on the other, and a deadline Friday. There is no public testimony. There is no hearing. There is a clock and a stack, and at some point after ten at night somebody says a number and somebody else says a smaller number.
Grange has been in that hallway since Tuesday morning. He is not in the room either. He does not have to be; he has been in it for thirty years.
PROCEDURE: Conference cannot add anything. It can only cut, merge, or let something die by running out the clock — and running out the clock is what it does most.
POLICY BRAIN: Everything gets decided against one figure now, and that figure has six hundred million dollars of storm in it. Your number is not competing with zero. It is competing with a bridge on Kauaʻi.
TALK STORY: The conferees are named. That is the entire public record you get. Every other thing you learn this week, you learn from somebody who should not have told you.
BASE BUILDING: This is the week the campaign either has durability or does not, and there is nothing left to do about which. Whatever you built by now is what walks into that room with your bill.

* Hold the number. No deal that cuts it, and say so out loud.
  {+5 support, -4 influence, flag held-the-number}
  You tell all six conferees, in writing, that the coalition will not support a diluted version. It is a real position honestly held and it is also a gun pointed at your own foot, and every person you hand it to understands both halves.
  -> friday

* Take the deal. Something at sixty percent beats nothing at a hundred.
  {+4 influence, -3 support, flag took-the-deal}
  You let it be known, quietly, that you can live with a smaller number. It is in front of the conferees within the hour, which tells you exactly how closely somebody was listening for it.
  -> friday

* [TALK STORY 13 red] Find Grange in the hallway and ask him what he is actually protecting.
  mods: influence/25, POLICY BRAIN/4
  pass:
    {+5 influence}
    He tells you, because at this hour of this week it costs him nothing and he has always slightly enjoyed you. It is not your bill. It is one clause in it, worth a fraction of the total, and he has been spending a week of a thirty-year reputation on it. You have been defending the whole thing against a man who only ever wanted one paragraph.
  fail:
    {-2 influence}
    "Long week," he says, and means it, and asks after your family, and means that too, and tells you nothing at all. He is very good at this. It is genuinely a pleasure to watch, right up until you are back in the hallway with what you came with.
  -> friday

--- friday
Friday. The conference deadline is tonight, and everything not agreed by midnight dies where it sits — no vote, no record, the same death as February, at the other end of the session.
You will find out in the morning. Everyone will.
PROCEDURE: One more beat after this: final reading, week sixteen. Nothing agreed tonight can be changed then. Whatever comes out of 309 is the bill.

* Wait it out in the hallway.
  {}
  You wait it out in the hallway, with everyone else who cannot go in.
